NAME
    tail - print the last lines

SYNOPSIS
    tail [-N] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the last N lines (default 10) of the files named, read as one
    stream (globs expand, "-" is stdin), or of stdin. The lines stream
    through a ring of 40 slots and are printed when the input ends. CR
    is dropped; each file ends a line. tail is /BIN/TAIL. stdin is the
    shell's < file or pipe (see shell), else the console up to Ctrl-D.

OPTIONS
    -N    the last N lines, 1..40 (e.g. -5); a count outside that range
          is clamped. A bare "-" is the console, not a count.

LIMITS
    Lines are cut at 255 characters.

EXAMPLES
    tail /FRUIT.TXT                  all 7 lines
    tail -3 /FRUIT.TXT /FRUIT2.TXT   the last 3 lines of FRUIT2.TXT
    tail -1 /MAN/TAIL                the last line of this page

NOTES
    The count is over the whole stream, not per file.
    Removed from the P8X version: input from a pipe or a < redirect.
    New: several files, globs and "-".
    Ported from P8X os/commands/tail.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    head, more, cat, stdin
